This is just level-order traversal on a graph. Every item in the queue belongs to the next possible distance from node `0`, so the distance can be assigned when the node is first visited.

The input is not changed. The edges are copied into an adjacency list with both directions added.

Dry run: for `0-1`, `1-2`, and `0-3`, node `0` gets distance `0`. The first queue round gives nodes `1` and `3` distance `1`. The next round gives node `2` distance `2`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public:
    vector<int> shortestPath(vector<vector<int>>& edges, int N,int M){
        vector<vector<int>> graph(N);

        for (const vector<int>& edge : edges) {
            int u = edge[0];
            int v = edge[1];
            graph[u].push_back(v);
            graph[v].push_back(u);
        }

        vector<int> distance(N, -1);
        queue<int> q;
        q.push(0);
        distance[0] = 0;

        while (!q.empty()) {
            int node = q.front();
            q.pop();

            for (int neighbor : graph[node]) {
                if (distance[neighbor] == -1) {
                    distance[neighbor] = distance[node] + 1;
                    q.push(neighbor);
                }
            }
        }

        return distance;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(N + M)`.

Space complexity: `O(N + M)`.

An empty graph with `N > 0` is also handled, because every node except `0` remains `-1`.

For this version, I use a boolean adjacency matrix. Since the graph is undirected, both `connected[u][v]` and `connected[v][u]` are set. BFS then checks every possible neighbor of the current node.

The matrix makes the neighbor check simple, but it uses more memory than an adjacency list. It can still be a reasonable choice when `N` is small.

Dry run: if node `0` connects to `1`, the queue starts with `0`, then `1` receives distance `1`. A later node connected through `1` receives distance `2`.

```go
package main

func shortestPath(edges [][]int, N int, M int) []int {
    connected := make([][]bool, N)
    for i := 0; i < N; i++ {
        connected[i] = make([]bool, N)
    }

    for _, edge := range edges {
        u, v := edge[0], edge[1]
        connected[u][v] = true
        connected[v][u] = true
    }

    distance := make([]int, N)
    for i := range distance {
        distance[i] = -1
    }

    queue := make([]int, 0, N)
    queue = append(queue, 0)
    distance[0] = 0

    for head := 0; head < len(queue); head++ {
        node := queue[head]

        for neighbor := 0; neighbor < N; neighbor++ {
            if connected[node][neighbor] && distance[neighbor] == -1 {
                distance[neighbor] = distance[node] + 1
                queue = append(queue, neighbor)
            }
        }
    }

    return distance
}
```

Time complexity: `O(N^2 + M)`.

Space complexity: `O(N^2)` for the matrix, plus `O(N)` for BFS data.

The code does not modify `edges`. It also returns `-1` for disconnected vertices.

The important detail is marking a node as soon as it enters the queue, not when it leaves it. That prevents the same vertex from being added many times. Because all edges have unit weight, adding one to the current distance is enough.

This uses an adjacency list and leaves the original edge list unchanged. A graph with one node works too, and its answer is simply `[0]`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public:
    vector<int> shortestPath(vector<vector<int>>& edges, int N,int M){
        vector<vector<int>> neighbors(N);

        for (int i = 0; i < M; i++) {
            int from = edges[i][0];
            int to = edges[i][1];
            neighbors[from].push_back(to);
            neighbors[to].push_back(from);
        }

        vector<int> answer(N, -1);
        vector<int> queue;
        queue.push_back(0);
        answer[0] = 0;

        int index = 0;
        while (index < static_cast<int>(queue.size())) {
            int current = queue[index++];

            for (int next : neighbors[current]) {
                if (answer[next] != -1) {
                    continue;
                }

                answer[next] = answer[current] + 1;
                queue.push_back(next);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(N + M)`.

Space complexity: `O(N + M)`.

The returned array gives the number of edges from node `0`. Any unreachable node keeps the value `-1`.

A graph list is not required here. The queue still stores the nodes reached by BFS, and for each node I scan the given edges to find its neighbors. When an edge touches the current node, the other endpoint can be visited.

This saves the separate adjacency-list construction, but each popped node may scan all `M` edges. It is easier to write, though slower on large graphs.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int[] shortestPath(int[][] edges, int N, int M) {
        int[] distance = new int[N];
        Arrays.fill(distance, -1);
        distance[0] = 0;

        Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(0);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int node = queue.poll();

            for (int i = 0; i < M; i++) {
                int u = edges[i][0];
                int v = edges[i][1];
                int next = -1;

                if (u == node) {
                    next = v;
                } else if (v == node) {
                    next = u;
                }

                if (next != -1 && distance[next] == -1) {
                    distance[next] = distance[node] + 1;
                    queue.offer(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return distance;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(N * M)` in the worst case, because every visited node scans all edges.

Space complexity: `O(N)` for the queue and distance array.

If `M` is zero, the result is `0` for node `0` and `-1` for all other nodes.
